\b[True.] Suppose a dual <feasible solution> $y$ exists. For every primal feasible $x$, <weak duality> gives $c^Tx\leq b^Ty$, a finite <upper bound> independent of $x$. This contradicts primal unboundedness. Therefore an unbounded primal has an infeasible dual. Here “unbounded” <arithmetic means> a nonempty feasible set with objective values unbounded above, rather than merely an unbounded feasible region.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

\b[False: both primal and dual can be infeasible.] For example, take
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad b=\binom{-1}{0},\qquad c=\binom10.
$$
The primal requires $0\leq-1$, so is infeasible. Its dual requires the first coordinate of $A^Ty$ to be at least $1$, namely $0\geq1$, and is also infeasible. An infeasible primal therefore does not force an unbounded dual. If the dual is additionally assumed feasible, <strong duality> rules out a finite dual optimum; a feasible <linear program> with finite optimal value attains it, so in that qualified case the dual is unbounded below.

